      </div>

      <h2 id="buying-guide">Buying Guide: Match Your Business Model to a Shopify Alternative</h2>
      <p>Do not migrate on fee math alone. Match your primary business model to the platform built for it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>WordPress / content-led / SEO-driven store</strong> → <strong>WooCommerce</strong>. Content + commerce on one domain is the reason to switch and the #1 SEO win vs Shopify subdomains.</li>
        <li><strong>High-volume / B2B / multi-brand / headless store</strong> → <strong>BigCommerce</strong>. Start on Standard; once you need multi-storefront or custom APIs, move to Plus/Enterprise.</li>
        <li><strong>Creator / editorial / design-led brand under ~300 SKUs</strong> → <strong>Squarespace Commerce Advanced</strong>. If you care about your homepage more than your app marketplace, this is the right move.</li>
        <li><strong>Absolute beginner / artist / solo creator with &lt;200 products</strong> → <strong>Wix Business VIP</strong>. Fastest store you can ship in a weekend without hiring anyone.</li>
        <li><strong>Existing website that just needs a cart / buy buttons</strong> → <strong>Ecwid</strong>. Free tier, embed anywhere, no replatforming. Treat it as a "Shopify Lite alternative."</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Common pitfalls: do not migrate a working Shopify store under ~$500k GMV for minor fee differences — migration and QA will eat the savings. Do not pick WooCommerce if nobody on your team knows WordPress. Do not pick Squarespace or Wix if your roadmap depends on 3+ niche Shopify apps. Do not pick BigCommerce Standard if you do not genuinely need its B2B and multi-storefront features.</p>
      <p>Learn more about our <a href="/how-we-test/">testing methodology</a> and <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Who should leave Shopify in 2026?</summary>
          <p>You should consider leaving Shopify in 2026 if any of these apply: (1) you are already a WordPress site owner and want to keep content + commerce on one platform instead of paying for two — WooCommerce is the natural fit; (2) you sell high-volume or enterprise SKUs (tens of thousands of products, B2B, headless) and BigCommerce's native multi-storefronts and open APIs save you hundreds of dollars per month vs Shopify Plus; (3) your brand is content-first, editorial, or creator-led and the site itself sells the product — Squarespace Commerce gives you better templates and built-in blogging; (4) you are a solo creator selling 10–100 digital or physical products and want simplicity plus design for under $35/month — Wix Ecommerce works great; or (5) you have an existing static site or CMS and just want to embed commerce without replatforming — Ecwid or Shopify Lite are the candidates. If you are happy with Shopify and your total fees are acceptable, there is no pressure to move.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which Shopify alternative has the lowest total fees?</summary>
          <p>WooCommerce self-hosted has the lowest total cost of ownership for merchants who are comfortable running a WordPress site. The core WooCommerce plugin is free, you pay only for hosting ($10–$60/mo depending on traffic) and optional premium extensions ($0–$500/year total for most stores). Squarespace Commerce Advanced and Wix Business have zero transaction fees on their built-in payments at ~$49/mo, undercutting Shopify Advanced's credit card + per-order fees above 1.0% of revenue. BigCommerce Standard undercuts Shopify on per-product fees for catalogs above ~$1M/yr GMV. At the enterprise end, BigCommerce Enterprise and headless WooCommerce on custom hosting typically beat Shopify Plus in year-1 TCO by 30–50% depending on complexity.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is WooCommerce better than Shopify for WordPress sites?</summary>
          <p>Yes, if your website already runs on WordPress or your business model is content-first (blog, content marketing, SEO-led traffic), WooCommerce is better than Shopify because it runs in the same WordPress instance, uses the same theme and CMS, and eliminates the subdomain or domain split that Shopify stores often suffer on. The full comparison of pricing, themes, plugins, SEO, and scaling trade-offs is in our Shopify vs WooCommerce review. For stores that are pure commerce with no blog-led traffic, Shopify is usually the easier default.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which hosted Shopify alternative has the best SEO?</summary>
          <p>BigCommerce has the strongest native SEO platform among Shopify alternatives because of its flexible URL structure, built-in blog engine, CDN and image optimization, SEO-rich product pages with structured data, plus headless APIs for custom frontends. Squarespace Commerce is a strong close second for brands that care about the content-to-commerce SEO connection because Squarespace ships best-in-class templates and a Fluid Engine editor for marketing content. Headless WooCommerce on WP Engine or Kinsta offers the highest SEO ceiling, with Rank Math Pro and other WordPress SEO plugins, but you pay that back in engineering and maintenance time.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

